{"name":"Coquina Shell","hex":"#bb9a88","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coquina","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several small marine clams, of the species Donax variabilis, common in United States coastal waters. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A soft form of limestone made of fragments of shells, sometimes used as a building or road paving material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coquina"},{"term":"shell","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard external covering of an animal.\nThe calcareous or chitinous external covering of mollusks, crustaceans, and some other invertebrates.","The calcareous or chitinous external covering of mollusks, crustaceans, and some other invertebrates."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To remove the outer covering or shell of something.","To bombard, to fire projectiles at, especially with artillery."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shell"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coquina","blocks":[{"text":"Coquina is a sedimentary rock that is composed either wholly or almost entirely of the transported, abraded, and mechanically sorted fragments of mollusks, trilobites, brachiopods, or other invertebrates. The term coquina comes from the Spanish word for \"cockle\" and \"shellfish\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Marine_terrace_64_%28Quaternary_-_Coquimbo_Fm.%29.jpg/330px-Marine_terrace_64_%28Quaternary_-_Coquimbo_Fm.%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coquina"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg/500px-Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg/1280px-Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.j